<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Grunwald</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1410">1410</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Tannenberg (1410), First Battle of Tannenberg, Battle of Žalgiris · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-teutonic-war">Polish–Lithuanian–Teutonic War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Grunwald, fought on 15 July 1410 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, was one of the largest battles of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. An allied army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> and Vytautas crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, killing Grand Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-von-jungingen">Ulrich von Jungingen</a> and much of the Order's leadership. The Order survived, but its military power never recovered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald.jpg" alt="Battle of Grunwald" width="320" height="143" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Matejko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Matejko,_Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vytautas suffered a heavy defeat against the Tatar commander Edigu at the Battle of the Vorskla River in 1399, but in 1410 his troops fought beside the Poles of Władysław II Jagiełło at the <b>Battle of Grunwald</b>, breaking the power of the Teutonic Order.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Order's military reputation collapsed at the <b>Battle of Grunwald</b> (Tannenberg) on 15 July 1410, when a Polish-Lithuanian army under Władysław II Jagiełło and Vytautas killed Grand Master Ulrich von Jungingen and much of the Order's leadership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the historian Jan Długosz, Polish knights sang it at the <b>Battle of Grunwald</b> in 1410 and before other notable battles in the years that followed; it also accompanied the coronation of Władysław III of Poland and of the first Jagiellonian kings.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bogurodzica">Bogurodzica (Mother of God)</a></li><li><blockquote>Together, Poland and Lithuania crushed the Teutonic Knights at <b>Grunwald</b> in 1410, and the Second Peace of Thorn in 1466 brought Royal Prussia and the port of Gdansk under the Crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1385-1569">Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569)</a></li><li><blockquote>Family members served as knights and administrators, and Heinrich von Plauen defended Marienburg after the <b>Battle of Grunwald</b> and became Grand Master of the State of the Teutonic Order.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-plauen">Lordship of Plauen</a></li><li><blockquote>Lingvenis, who ruled from about 1390 to 1431, also served as a commander in Novgorod and fought at <b>Grunwald</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-mstislavl">Principality of Mstislavl</a></li></ul></section>
